data: {"type": "sources", "sources": [{"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:0", "excerpt": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562 ¶ Source code: Lib/uuid.py This module provides immutable UUID objects (the UUID class) and functions for generating UUIDs corresponding to a specific UUID version as specified in RFC 9562 (which supersedes RFC 4122 ), for example, uuid1() for UUID version 1, uuid3() for UUID version 3, and so on. Note that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC. If all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call uuid1() or uuid4() . Note that uuid1() may compromise privacy since it creates a UUID containing the computer’s network address. uuid4() creates a random UUID. Depending on support from the underlying platform, uuid1() may or may not return a “safe” UUID. A safe UUID is one which is generated using synchronization methods that ensure no two processes can obtain the same UUID. All instances of UUID have an", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}, {"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:14", "excerpt": "# make a UUID based on the host ID and current time >>> uuid . uuid1 () UUID('a8098c1a-f86e-11da-bd1a-00112444be1e') >>> # make a UUID using an MD5 hash of a namespace UUID and a name >>> uuid . uuid3 ( uuid . NAMESPACE_DNS , 'python.org' ) UUID('6fa459ea-ee8a-3ca4-894e-db77e160355e') >>> # make a random UUID >>> uuid . uuid4 () UUID('16fd2706-8baf-433b-82eb-8c7fada847da') >>> # make a UUID using a SHA-1 hash of a namespace UUID and a name >>> uuid . uuid5 ( uuid . NAMESPACE_DNS , 'python.org' ) UUID('886313e1-3b8a-5372-9b90-0c9aee199e5d') >>> # make a UUID from a string of hex digits (braces and hyphens ignored) >>> x = uuid . UUID ( '{00010203-0405-0607-0809-0a0b0c0d0e0f}' ) >>> # convert a UUID to a string of hex digits in standard form >>> str ( x ) '00010203-0405-0607-0809-0a0b0c0d0e0f' >>> # get the raw 16 bytes of the UUID >>> x . bytes", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}, {"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:6", "excerpt": "meaningful only when the variant is RFC_4122 ). Changed in version 3.14: Added UUID versions 6, 7 and 8. UUID. is_safe ¶ An enumeration of SafeUUID which indicates whether the platform generated the UUID in a multiprocessing-safe way. Added in version 3.7. The uuid module defines the following functions: uuid. getnode ( ) ¶ Get the hardware address as a 48-bit positive integer. The first time this runs, it may launch a separate program, which could be quite slow. If all attempts to obtain the hardware address fail, we choose a random 48-bit number with the multicast bit (least significant bit of the first octet) set to 1 as recommended in RFC 4122 . “Hardware address” means the MAC address of a network interface. On a machine with multiple network interfaces, universally administered MAC addresses (i.e. where the second least significant bit of the first octet is unset", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}, {"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:7", "excerpt": "erfaces, universally administered MAC addresses (i.e. where the second least significant bit of the first octet is unset ) will be preferred over locally administered MAC addresses, but with no other ordering guarantees. Changed in version 3.7: Universally administered MAC addresses are preferred over locally administered MAC addresses, since the former are guaranteed to be globally unique, while the latter are not. uuid. uuid1 ( node = None , clock_seq = None ) ¶ Generate a UUID from a host ID, sequence number, and the current time according to RFC 9562, §5.1 . When node is not specified, getnode() is used to obtain the hardware address as a 48-bit positive integer. When a sequence number clock_seq is not specified, a pseudo-random 14-bit positive integer is generated. If node or clock_seq exceed their expected bit count, only their least significant bits are kept. uuid. uuid3 (", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}, {"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:8", "excerpt": "nerated. If node or clock_seq exceed their expected bit count, only their least significant bits are kept. uuid. uuid3 ( namespace , name ) ¶ Generate a UUID based on the MD5 hash of a namespace identifier (which is a UUID) and a name (which is a bytes object or a string that will be encoded using UTF-8) according to RFC 9562, §5.3 . uuid. uuid4 ( ) ¶ Generate a random UUID in a cryptographically-secure method according to RFC 9562, §5.4 . uuid. uuid5 ( namespace , name ) ¶ Generate a UUID based on the SHA-1 hash of a namespace identifier (which is a UUID) and a name (which is a bytes object or a string that will be encoded using UTF-8) according to RFC 9562, §5.5 . uuid. uuid6 ( node = None , clock_seq = None ) ¶ Generate a UUID from a sequence number and the current time according to RFC 9562, §5.6 . This is an alternative to uuid1() to improve database locality. When node", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}]}

data: {"type": "meta", "intent": "answer", "recommendations": [{"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z"}], "generation_mode": "generating", "provisional": true, "citation_validation": {"status": "pending", "semantic_grounding": "not_verified"}}

data: {"type": "text", "text": "Trích dẫn nguyên văn từ nguồn (không phải diễn giải):\n\n> ```text\n> meaningful only when the variant is RFC_4122 ). Changed in version 3.14: Added UUID versions 6, 7 and 8. UUID. is_safe ¶ An enumeration of SafeUUID which indicates whether the platform generated the UUID in a multiprocessing-safe way. Added in version 3.7. The uuid module defines the following functions: uuid. getnode ( ) ¶ Get the hardware address as a 48-bit positive integer. The first time this runs, it may launch a separate program, which could be quite slow. If all attempts to obtain the hardware address fail, we choose a random 48-bit number with the multicast bit (least significant bit of the first octet) set to 1 as recommended in RFC 4122 . “Hardware address” means the MAC address of a network interface. On a machine with multiple network interfaces, universally administered MAC addresses (i.e. where the second least significant bit of the first octet is unset\n> ```\n\n[item_id:5064] [chunk_id:5064:6]\n\n> ```text\n> nerated. If node or clock_seq exceed their expected bit count, only their least significant bits are kept. uuid. uuid3 ( namespace , name ) ¶ Generate a UUID based on the MD5 hash of a namespace identifier (which is a UUID) and a name (which is a bytes object or a string that will be encoded using UTF-8) according to RFC 9562, §5.3 . uuid. uuid4 ( ) ¶ Generate a random UUID in a cryptographically-secure method according to RFC 9562, §5.4 . uuid. uuid5 ( namespace , name ) ¶ Generate a UUID based on the SHA-1 hash of a namespace identifier (which is a UUID) and a name (which is a bytes object or a string that will be encoded using UTF-8) according to RFC 9562, §5.5 . uuid. uuid6 ( node = None , clock_seq = None ) ¶ Generate a UUID from a sequence number and the current time according to RFC 9562, §5.6 . This is an alternative to uuid1() to improve database locality. When node\n> ```\n\n[item_id:5064] [chunk_id:5064:8]"}

data: {"type": "final", "response": "Trích dẫn nguyên văn từ nguồn (không phải diễn giải):\n\n> ```text\n> meaningful only when the variant is RFC_4122 ). Changed in version 3.14: Added UUID versions 6, 7 and 8. UUID. is_safe ¶ An enumeration of SafeUUID which indicates whether the platform generated the UUID in a multiprocessing-safe way. Added in version 3.7. The uuid module defines the following functions: uuid. getnode ( ) ¶ Get the hardware address as a 48-bit positive integer. The first time this runs, it may launch a separate program, which could be quite slow. If all attempts to obtain the hardware address fail, we choose a random 48-bit number with the multicast bit (least significant bit of the first octet) set to 1 as recommended in RFC 4122 . “Hardware address” means the MAC address of a network interface. On a machine with multiple network interfaces, universally administered MAC addresses (i.e. where the second least significant bit of the first octet is unset\n> ```\n\n[item_id:5064] [chunk_id:5064:6]\n\n> ```text\n> nerated. If node or clock_seq exceed their expected bit count, only their least significant bits are kept. uuid. uuid3 ( namespace , name ) ¶ Generate a UUID based on the MD5 hash of a namespace identifier (which is a UUID) and a name (which is a bytes object or a string that will be encoded using UTF-8) according to RFC 9562, §5.3 . uuid. uuid4 ( ) ¶ Generate a random UUID in a cryptographically-secure method according to RFC 9562, §5.4 . uuid. uuid5 ( namespace , name ) ¶ Generate a UUID based on the SHA-1 hash of a namespace identifier (which is a UUID) and a name (which is a bytes object or a string that will be encoded using UTF-8) according to RFC 9562, §5.5 . uuid. uuid6 ( node = None , clock_seq = None ) ¶ Generate a UUID from a sequence number and the current time according to RFC 9562, §5.6 . This is an alternative to uuid1() to improve database locality. When node\n> ```\n\n[item_id:5064] [chunk_id:5064:8]", "intent": "answer", "sources": [{"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:0", "excerpt": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562 ¶ Source code: Lib/uuid.py This module provides immutable UUID objects (the UUID class) and functions for generating UUIDs corresponding to a specific UUID version as specified in RFC 9562 (which supersedes RFC 4122 ), for example, uuid1() for UUID version 1, uuid3() for UUID version 3, and so on. Note that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC. If all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call uuid1() or uuid4() . Note that uuid1() may compromise privacy since it creates a UUID containing the computer’s network address. uuid4() creates a random UUID. Depending on support from the underlying platform, uuid1() may or may not return a “safe” UUID. A safe UUID is one which is generated using synchronization methods that ensure no two processes can obtain the same UUID. All instances of UUID have an", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}, {"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:14", "excerpt": "# make a UUID based on the host ID and current time >>> uuid . uuid1 () UUID('a8098c1a-f86e-11da-bd1a-00112444be1e') >>> # make a UUID using an MD5 hash of a namespace UUID and a name >>> uuid . uuid3 ( uuid . NAMESPACE_DNS , 'python.org' ) UUID('6fa459ea-ee8a-3ca4-894e-db77e160355e') >>> # make a random UUID >>> uuid . uuid4 () UUID('16fd2706-8baf-433b-82eb-8c7fada847da') >>> # make a UUID using a SHA-1 hash of a namespace UUID and a name >>> uuid . uuid5 ( uuid . NAMESPACE_DNS , 'python.org' ) UUID('886313e1-3b8a-5372-9b90-0c9aee199e5d') >>> # make a UUID from a string of hex digits (braces and hyphens ignored) >>> x = uuid . UUID ( '{00010203-0405-0607-0809-0a0b0c0d0e0f}' ) >>> # convert a UUID to a string of hex digits in standard form >>> str ( x ) '00010203-0405-0607-0809-0a0b0c0d0e0f' >>> # get the raw 16 bytes of the UUID >>> x . bytes", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}, {"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:6", "excerpt": "meaningful only when the variant is RFC_4122 ). Changed in version 3.14: Added UUID versions 6, 7 and 8. UUID. is_safe ¶ An enumeration of SafeUUID which indicates whether the platform generated the UUID in a multiprocessing-safe way. Added in version 3.7. The uuid module defines the following functions: uuid. getnode ( ) ¶ Get the hardware address as a 48-bit positive integer. The first time this runs, it may launch a separate program, which could be quite slow. If all attempts to obtain the hardware address fail, we choose a random 48-bit number with the multicast bit (least significant bit of the first octet) set to 1 as recommended in RFC 4122 . “Hardware address” means the MAC address of a network interface. On a machine with multiple network interfaces, universally administered MAC addresses (i.e. where the second least significant bit of the first octet is unset", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}, {"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:7", "excerpt": "erfaces, universally administered MAC addresses (i.e. where the second least significant bit of the first octet is unset ) will be preferred over locally administered MAC addresses, but with no other ordering guarantees. Changed in version 3.7: Universally administered MAC addresses are preferred over locally administered MAC addresses, since the former are guaranteed to be globally unique, while the latter are not. uuid. uuid1 ( node = None , clock_seq = None ) ¶ Generate a UUID from a host ID, sequence number, and the current time according to RFC 9562, §5.1 . When node is not specified, getnode() is used to obtain the hardware address as a 48-bit positive integer. When a sequence number clock_seq is not specified, a pseudo-random 14-bit positive integer is generated. If node or clock_seq exceed their expected bit count, only their least significant bits are kept. uuid. uuid3 (", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}, {"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z", "citation_id": "item_id:5064", "chunk_id": "5064:8", "excerpt": "nerated. If node or clock_seq exceed their expected bit count, only their least significant bits are kept. uuid. uuid3 ( namespace , name ) ¶ Generate a UUID based on the MD5 hash of a namespace identifier (which is a UUID) and a name (which is a bytes object or a string that will be encoded using UTF-8) according to RFC 9562, §5.3 . uuid. uuid4 ( ) ¶ Generate a random UUID in a cryptographically-secure method according to RFC 9562, §5.4 . uuid. uuid5 ( namespace , name ) ¶ Generate a UUID based on the SHA-1 hash of a namespace identifier (which is a UUID) and a name (which is a bytes object or a string that will be encoded using UTF-8) according to RFC 9562, §5.5 . uuid. uuid6 ( node = None , clock_seq = None ) ¶ Generate a UUID from a sequence number and the current time according to RFC 9562, §5.6 . This is an alternative to uuid1() to improve database locality. When node", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "provenance": "crawl:python-official", "evidence_kind": "source_text"}], "recommendations": [{"item_id": 5064, "title": "uuid — UUID objects according to RFC 9562", "type": "Tài liệu", "level": "Trung cấp", "description": "uuid\n— UUID objects according to\nRFC 9562\n¶\nSource code:\nLib/uuid.py\nThis module provides immutable\nUUID\nobjects (the\nUUID\nclass)\nand\nfunctions\nfor generating UUIDs corresponding\nto a specific UUID version as specified in\nRFC 9562\n(which supersedes\nRFC 4122\n),\nfor example,\nuuid1()\nfor UUID version 1,\nuuid3()\nfor UUID version 3, and so on.\nNote that UUID version 2 is deliberately omitted as it is outside the scope of the RFC.\nIf all you want is a unique ID, you should probably call\nuuid1()\nor\nuuid4()\n.  Note that\nuuid1()\nmay compromise privacy since it creates\na UUID containing the computer’s n", "category": "Lập trình", "topics": "python,programming", "instructor": "Python Software Foundation", "platform": "Python Software Foundation", "link": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/uuid.html", "origin": "crawl:python-official", "crawl_license": "PSF-2.0; https://docs.python.org/3/license.html", "crawl_lang": "en", "crawl_hash": "ed2028bd8254f6f2eb38493c0666723d2077a571a67cc44bd7f0164e87ee1d00", "source_updated_at": null, "source_fetched_at": "2026-10-29T14:27:52Z"}], "provisional": false, "generation_mode": "llm_extractive", "generation_error": null, "citation_validation": {"status": "valid", "cited_ids": [5064], "invalid_ids": [], "malformed": false, "invalid_chunk_ids": [], "missing_chunk_item_ids": [], "semantic_grounding": "not_verified", "evidence_binding": "verbatim"}}

